          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is generalized anxiety disorder, and how is it different from ordinary worry?",
          "answer": "Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD) is a condition where a person worries persistently about a wide range of everyday matters. The worry happens on more days than not, lasts at least six months, and is hard to control. It is also linked with physical and mental symptoms. (Source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder,\" General clinical reference.)\n\nOrdinary worry and GAD differ in a few ways, according to the same source:\n\n- **Breadth:** GAD worry moves from topic to topic. It does not stay attached to one situation.\n- **Proportion:** Anxiety is a normal response to threat. It becomes a disorder when it is out of proportion to the circumstances, keeps going after the circumstances change, and gets in the way of daily life.\n- **Accompanying symptoms:** Restlessness, tiring easily, trouble concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and disturbed sleep are commonly described alongside the worry.\n\nThe reference I searched did not cover how GAD is assessed, its causes, or its treatment options. If you are worried about your own worry or sleep, a doctor or other clinician is the right person to talk to. They can discuss what you are experiencing and whether an assessment makes sense.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
